Abstract = {An experiment was conducted to assess the effect of victim tolerance,
   harasser performance status, and decision maker sex on reactions to a
   complaint of sexual harassment. Judges' reactions were less favorable
   toward the complainant when she had endured the harassment and when her
   harasser was an above-average performer. Also, relative to males, female
   decision makers' reactions were more favorable toward the complainant
   and less favorable toward the perpetrator. The implications of the
   findings for training and organizational efforts to counter sexual
   harassment in the workplace are discussed. (C) 1996 Academic Press, Inc.},
